{"reply":"A useful shift might be to treat your role as making options easier, not deciding the outcome. Before moving or arranging anything, you could ask: “Would you like ideas, hands-on help, or just company while you decide?”

Then try offering one small, reversible suggestion at a time: “I could put the books here if that would help—what do you think?” If she hesitates, leave the choice open rather than persuading her. You might also make a personal rule: don’t arrange shared or visible spaces without asking first; for practical tasks, ask which parts she wants you to take over.

It makes sense that your good intention and your influence can overlap. Leaving the choices with her doesn’t mean being less helpful—it means letting her define what “settled” feels like. What would it look like to help in a way that reduces effort without shaping the flat around your preferences?","memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}
